  "theory": {
        "intro": "The radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\;}$ denotes the **principal (non-negative) square root**, so $\\sqrt{A} \\ge 0$ always. Squaring a negative number makes it positive before taking the root, giving the identity $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|$ for all real numbers $x$.",
        "facts": [
          "The **principal square root** is always non-negative: $\\sqrt{A} \\ge 0$ for all $A \\ge 0$.",
          "Fundamental identity: $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|$ for all real numbers $x$.",
          "When $x < 0$, $\\sqrt{x^2} = -x \\neq x$ because the root must be positive.",
          "For any algebraic expression $f(x)$, $\\sqrt{(f(x))^2} = |f(x)|$.",
          "For scaling factors: $\\sqrt{a^2 x^2} = |a||x|$, which simplifies to $a|x|$ when $a > 0$.",
          "For perfect square trinomials: $\\sqrt{x^2 \\pm 2kx + k^2} = \\sqrt{(x \\pm k)^2} = |x \\pm k|$."
        ]
      },
